<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Negeri Sembilan</h1><p class="st-meta">1773 CE – present (approx.) · capital Seri Menanti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Confederation of nine Minangkabau-settled territories in the Malay Peninsula, with an elected ruler and matrilineal customary law.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minangkabau-people">Minangkabau</a> migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> settled the hills inland from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> from the 15th century onward, forming small territories (luak) under their own chiefs. In 1773 the chiefs invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-melewar">Raja Melewar</a>, a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagaruyung">Pagaruyung</a> royal house, to become their overlord, and he was installed as the first Yang di-Pertuan Besar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seri-menanti">Seri Menanti</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ruler was chosen by the four great territorial chiefs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/undang">Undang</a>, rather than succeeding automatically. Society followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a> perpatih, a matrilineal custom in which land passed through women and clans chose their leaders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minangkabau-people">Minangkabau</a> architecture with curved roofs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mining in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sungai-ujong">Sungai Ujong</a>, marked the region.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Rivalries over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> brought British intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sungai-ujong">Sungai Ujong</a> in 1874; the confederation was reconstituted under British protection in 1895 and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-malay-states">Federated Malay States</a>. It is now a state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a>, and its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuanku-abdul-rahman">Tuanku Abdul Rahman</a> became the first king of independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Malaya</a> in 1957.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/negeri-sembilan.jpg" alt="Negeri Sembilan" width="320" height="122"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Negeri_Sembilan_in_Malaysia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1773 CE – present (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Seri Menanti</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Raja Melewar, Tuanku Abdul Rahman, Tuanku Muhriz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Negeri Sembilan Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Negri Sembilan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1900&amp;focus=negeri-sembilan" aria-label="Negeri Sembilan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7516 2077 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Negeri Sembilan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7836 2333L7881 2378M7836 2333L4997 651M7836 2333L7823 2316M7836 2333L7823 2316"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7881 2378h0M4997 651h0M7823 2316h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7836 2333h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7836 2333h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1900&amp;focus=negeri-sembilan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
